## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to derive nested-loop complexity where inner bound shrinks with i, revealing a harmonic-sum growth.

## Key Concepts
- Harmonic series: sum floor(n/i) ~ n log n
- Theta vs O, space O(1)
- Closed-form approximations vs exact sums

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPairs(n){
  let count=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let j=1;j<=Math.floor(n/i);j++){
      count++;
    }
  }
  return count;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you prove the Theta(n log n) bound?
- How does changing inner bound to floor(n/i^2) affect complexity?
- What are practical implications for large n?

## Why This Is Asked
Maintaining the number of distinct substrings under incremental appends tests knowledge of suffix automata, amortized analysis, and practical update strategies. It highlights how to avoid recomputation and reason about state growth.

## Key Concepts
- Suffix Automaton (SAM) for distinct substrings
- Online extensions and delta = len(cur) - len(link(cur))
- Amortized analysis over batches
- Space linear in string length

## Code Example
```javascript
class SAMNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = {};
    this.link = -1;
    this.len = 0;
  }
}
class SuffixAutomaton {
  constructor() {
    this.st = [new SAMNode()];
    this.last = 0;
    this.size = 1;
  }
  extend(c){
    const st = this.st; const cur = this.size++;
    st.push(new SAMNode()); st[cur].len = st[this.last].len + 1;
    let p = this.last;
    while (p >= 0 && !(c in st[p].next)) { st[p].next[c] = cur; p = st[p].link; }
    if (p === -1) { st[cur].link = 0; }
    else {
      const q = st[p].next[c];
      if (st[p].len + 1 === st[q].len) {
        st[cur].link = q;
      } else {
        const clone = this.size++;
        st.push(Object.assign(new SAMNode(), st[q]));
        st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
        while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] === q) { st[p].next[c] = clone; p = st[p].link; }
        st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
      }
    }
    this.last = cur;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle a large alphabet or non-text streams efficiently?
- How does the approach adapt if deletions are allowed?
- What are practical bottlenecks in real-world languages?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive amortized costs for dynamic arrays and relate growth strategies to real hardware effects.

## Key Concepts

- Amortized analysis
- Geometric series and resizing costs
- Growth factor impact on performance
- Cache locality during bulk copies

## Code Example

```javascript
let cap = 1, n = 0, arr = new Array(cap);
function append(x) {
  if (n === cap) {
    const newCap = cap * 2;
    const tmp = new Array(newCap);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) tmp[i] = arr[i];
    arr = tmp; cap = newCap;
  }
  arr[n++] = x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure the actual runtime and memory in a real language? 
- How does adaptive growth (non-constant factor) affect cache misses during resizes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design incremental single-source shortest paths with batch updates and to reason about amortized work and practical data structures.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental SSSP with batch edge insertions
- Localized relaxation and pruning
- Priority queues and potential bucketed variants (Dial's)
- Amortized analysis and space efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
function updateBatch(batchEdges){
  for (const [u,v,w] of batchEdges){
    // ensure undirected view if needed
    adj[u].push([v,w]);
    adj[v].push([u,w]);
  }
  for (const [u,v,w] of batchEdges){
    if (dist[v] > dist[u] + w){
      dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
      pq.push([dist[v], v]);
    }
    if (dist[u] > dist[v] + w){
      dist[u] = dist[v] + w;
      pq.push([dist[u], u]);
    }
  }
  while (!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d, x] = pq.pop();
    if (d !== dist[x]) continue;
    for (const [y,w] of adj[x]){
      if (dist[y] > dist[x] + w){
        dist[y] = dist[x] + w;
        pq.push([dist[y], y]);
      }
    }
  }
}
```
